**Goal** — Add the durable job contract and atomic per-project record storage.

**Motivation** — That stitching is the pain the roadmap exists to remove.

**Approach** — New `src/chat/jobs/job-types.ts` with `ChatJobKind`, `ChatJobState`, `ChatJob` and `JOB_ID_PATTERN`, and new `src/chat/jobs/job-store.ts` with `CHAT_JOBS_DIR`, `jobsDir`, `jobLogPath`, `newJobId`, `writeJobRecord` and `readJobRecords`, as in Design § Data shapes. `writeJobRecord` writes `<id>.json` through a temp file and a rename, so a reader never sees half a record. `readJobRecords` keeps only records with `schemaVersion` 1, a string `id` matching `JOB_ID_PATTERN`, a known `kind` and `state`, a numeric `pid`, and string `cwd`, `startedAt`, `logPath` and `sessionId`. `_jobStoreDeps.randomHex` (default: 8 hex characters from `crypto.randomUUID()`) makes `newJobId` testable. Tests go in a new `test/unit/chat/jobs/job-store.test.ts`, using a temp directory.

Job records live in the project output dir: `<outputDir>/chat-jobs/<id>.json`, with the log at `<outputDir>/chat-jobs/<id>.log`. `outputDir` is the value `chat-host.ts` already computes (`projectOutputDir(getProjectKey(config, repoRoot), config.outputDir)`), so jobs are per project and a later chat in the same repo finds them. A job record file that is not JSON or fails the shape guard is skipped; a missing directory yields `[]`.

**Scope** — In: the record types, path/id helpers, atomic writes, shape filtering and newest-first reads. Out: argv parsing (US-002), outcome resolution (US-003), runner lifecycle (US-005–US-007), provider and host composition (US-009–US-013).

**Interface** —
```ts
export type ChatJobKind = "plan" | "run" | "bot_review" | "bot_fix";
export type ChatJobState = "running" | "succeeded" | "failed" | "cancelled" | "lost";

export interface ChatJob {
  readonly schemaVersion: 1;
  /** `<kind>-<8 lowercase hex>`, e.g. `run-1a2b3c4d`. */
  readonly id: string;
  readonly kind: ChatJobKind;
  /** Feature name for plan/run; the PR number as a decimal string for bot jobs. */
  readonly target: string;
  readonly argv: readonly string[];
  /** The repo root the job runs in. */
  readonly cwd: string;
  readonly pid: number;
  readonly startedAt: string;
  readonly endedAt?: string;
  readonly state: ChatJobState;
  readonly logPath: string;
  /** The chat session that started it. */
  readonly sessionId: string;
  /** When the user said yes on the launch card. */
  readonly approvedAt: string;
  readonly cancelRequested?: true;
  readonly exitCode?: number;
  readonly cost?: number;
  readonly progress?: { readonly passed: number; readonly total: number };
  readonly note?: string;
}

export const JOB_ID_PATTERN = /^(plan|run|bot_review|bot_fix)-[0-9a-f]{8}$/;

export const CHAT_JOBS_DIR = "chat-jobs";
export function jobsDir(outputDir: string): string;            // join(outputDir, "chat-jobs")
export function jobLogPath(outputDir: string, id: string): string; // join(jobsDir, `${id}.log`)
export function newJobId(kind: ChatJobKind): string;           // via _jobStoreDeps.randomHex
export function writeJobRecord(outputDir: string, job: ChatJob): Promise<void>;
export function readJobRecords(outputDir: string): Promise<ChatJob[]>; // newest first by startedAt
```

**Goal** — Add pure validation and shell-free argv construction for the four launch kinds.

**Motivation** — That stitching is the pain the roadmap exists to remove.

**Approach** — New `src/chat/jobs/job-argv.ts` with `JobLaunchInput`, `parseJobInput` and `buildJobArgv`, as in Design § Data shapes, with the validation rows of Design § Failure Handling that concern input fields. Pure: no file reads. Tests go in a new `test/unit/chat/jobs/job-argv.test.ts`.

Argv, exactly, with `E` = `...naxEntry`, `R` = `repoRoot` and `P` = one `"--profile", <p>` pair per profile in order:
- plan: `[E, "plan", "-f", feature, "--from", spec, "-d", R, P]`
- run: `[E, "run", "-f", feature, "-d", R, "--headless", P]`, then `"-m", String(maxIterations)` when set, then `"--max-cost", String(maxCost)` when set
- bot_review: `[E, "bot", "review", String(pr), "-d", R, P]`, then `"--model", model` when set, then `"--decisions", decisions` when set
- bot_fix: `[E, "bot", "fix", String(pr), "-d", R, P]`, then `"--model", model` when set, then `"--trust"` when `trust` is true, then `"--no-rereview"` when `noRereview` is true

Failure handling: missing/non-string feature → `[chat] feature must be a string`; preserve validateFeatureName's thrown message. Profiles default to []; a non-array or any non-string member → `[chat] profiles must be an array of strings`; names must match `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$`, else `[chat] profile <p> is not a valid profile name`. maxIterations must be an integer from 1 to 1000, else `[chat] maxIterations must be an integer from 1 to 1000`. maxCost must be finite and above 0, else `[chat] maxCost must be a positive number`. pr must be a positive integer, else `[chat] pr must be a positive integer`; the explicit AC requires numeric string `"12"` to normalize to 12. Model must match `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._:/-]*$`, else `[chat] model <m> is not a valid model id`. spec/decisions must be repo-relative, not absolute, not starting `-`, and contain no `..` segment, else `[chat] <field> must be a repo-relative path`. trust/noRereview, when present, must be booleans, else `[chat] <field> must be a boolean`.

**Scope** — In: parse input and construct exact arrays without file reads. Out: featureDir/target existence checks and resolving a default plan spec (US-009/US-010), cards (US-008/US-009), busy checking and spawning (US-005/US-010). Do not expose the CLI flags excluded at feature level.

**Interface** —
```ts
export type JobLaunchInput =
  | { readonly kind: "plan"; readonly feature: string; readonly spec?: string; readonly profiles: readonly string[] }
  | { readonly kind: "run"; readonly feature: string; readonly profiles: readonly string[];
      readonly maxIterations?: number; readonly maxCost?: number }
  | { readonly kind: "bot_review"; readonly pr: number; readonly profiles: readonly string[];
      readonly model?: string; readonly decisions?: string }
  | { readonly kind: "bot_fix"; readonly pr: number; readonly profiles: readonly string[];
      readonly model?: string; readonly trust?: boolean; readonly noRereview?: boolean };

/** Checks `feature` with `validateFeatureName` only; `featureDir` needs a repo root and is called by jobs-launch. */
export function parseJobInput(kind: ChatJobKind, raw: Record<string, unknown>): JobLaunchInput | { readonly error: string };
/** `spec` must be resolved (repo-relative) before a plan argv is built. */
export function buildJobArgv(input: JobLaunchInput & { readonly spec?: string },
                             ctx: { readonly naxEntry: readonly string[]; readonly repoRoot: string }): readonly string[];
```

**Goal** — Determine job completion from run/plan records and expose PID-matched run progress.

**Motivation** — That stitching is the pain the roadmap exists to remove.

**Approach** — New `src/chat/jobs/job-outcome.ts` with `JobOutcome`, `resolveJobOutcome` and `readRunProgress`, following the outcome rules in Design § Data shapes. `_jobOutcomeDeps = { readStatusFile, stat }`. `readStatusFile` is imported by relative leaf path. Tests go in a new `test/unit/chat/jobs/job-outcome.test.ts`, with temp-dir `status.json` and `prd.json` fixtures. It reaches `src/execution/status-file-read.ts` by relative leaf path (`../../execution/status-file-read`), never through `@/execution`, as feature 1 does.

Outcome rules, applied in this order:
1. `job.cancelRequested` → `cancelled`. For a run whose status record matches (rule 2), `cost` and `progress` are kept.
2. run: `status = readStatusFile(featureDir(repoRoot, job.target))`. When `status.run.pid === job.pid`: `completed` → `succeeded`; `aborted` → `cancelled`; any other status → `failed` with note `run status <status>`. `cost = status.cost.spent`, `progress = { passed, total }` from `status.progress`. When `completed` and `exit.exitCode` is defined and non-zero, the state stays `succeeded` with note `exit code <n> disagrees with the run record`. With no status, or a status whose pid differs → `failed` with note `no run record for pid <pid>`, followed by `; exit code <n>` when the exit code is known.
3. plan: `<featureDir>/prd.json` exists and its modification time is at or after `Date.parse(job.startedAt)` → `succeeded`; otherwise `failed` with note `plan wrote no prd.json`.
4. bot_review / bot_fix: exit code `0` → `succeeded`; a non-zero exit code → `failed` with note `exit code <n>`; no exit code → `lost` with note `outcome unknown: exit code not observed`.

`readRunProgress` returns `{ cost, progress }` from the same status file when its `run.pid` is the job's pid, else `null`.

**Scope** — In: pure outcome decisions around injectable status/stat reads and progress projection. Out: observing subprocess exits, persisting terminal records and notices (US-005/US-006), signalling or cancellation identity checks (US-007), changing the existing status reader. Cost for plan and bot jobs; only `run` jobs report cost, from `status.json`.

**Interface** —
```ts
export interface JobOutcome {
  readonly state: Exclude<ChatJobState, "running">;
  readonly cost?: number;
  readonly progress?: { readonly passed: number; readonly total: number };
  readonly note?: string;
}
export function resolveJobOutcome(job: ChatJob, exit: { readonly exitCode?: number }, repoRoot: string): Promise<JobOutcome>;
export function readRunProgress(job: ChatJob, repoRoot: string):
  Promise<{ readonly cost: number; readonly progress: { readonly passed: number; readonly total: number } } | null>;
```

**Goal** — Centralize model-facing job notices and usage instructions in the existing workflow prompt builder.

**Motivation** — That stitching is the pain the roadmap exists to remove.

**Approach** — `src/prompts/builders/workflow-builder.ts` gains `JobNoticeInput`, `buildJobNotice` and `buildJobsInstructions`, re-exported from `src/prompts/index.ts`. The builders take plain values and import nothing from `src/chat/`. Tests go in the existing `test/unit/prompts/builders/workflow-builder.test.ts`.

`buildJobNotice` returns `[nax-jobs] job <id> (<kind> <target>) ended: <state>`, then ` cost $<cost.toFixed(2)>` when `cost` is set, then ` (<note>)` when `note` is set, then ` log <logPath>`, then a newline and one line telling the agent to call `nax-workflow__state`, report the outcome to the user and propose the single next step.

`buildJobsInstructions` content (exact wording is the builder's):
1. names `nax-jobs__plan`, `nax-jobs__run`, `nax-jobs__bot_review` and `nax-jobs__bot_fix`, and says each is billed and shows the user an approval card;
2. says a denial is final for that call, and the agent must not retry with changed arguments to get round it;
3. says jobs run in the background and a `[nax-jobs]` notice arrives when one ends;
4. says Write, Edit, Delete and GitCommit are refused in this checkout while a run or plan job runs;
5. says to start one step at a time: after a job ends, report and propose the next step.

**Scope** — In: additive pure builders, barrel exports, failure-notice formatting, and preservation of the existing `buildWorkflowInstructions` output. Out: runner notice queuing (US-005), provider instructions attachment (US-011), notice turns (US-014). Do not rewrite `buildWorkflowInstructions`; its text is unchanged. Full-run (chain) approval and the agent starting a second billed step without a new card; this is feature `chat-workflow-chain`.

**Interface** —
```ts
export interface JobNoticeInput {
  readonly id: string; readonly kind: string; readonly target: string; readonly state: string;
  readonly cost?: number; readonly note?: string; readonly logPath: string;
}
export function buildJobNotice(input: JobNoticeInput): string;
export function buildJobsInstructions(): string;
```

**Goal** — Add the detached job runner's start/owned-exit lifecycle and FIFO completion notices.

**Motivation** — That stitching is the pain the roadmap exists to remove.

**Approach** — New `src/chat/jobs/job-runner.ts` with `JobRunnerOptions`, `StartJobRequest`, `ChatJobs`, `createJobRunner`, the three constants and `_jobRunnerDeps`, behaving as described under Design § Data shapes for `start`, owned-child exit, `list`, `get`, `running`, `owned`, `peekNotice`, `takeNotice` and `onChange`. Spawn options: `cwd: repoRoot`, `detached: true`, `stdin: "ignore"`, and one append fd for `jobLogPath(outputDir, id)` passed as both `stdout` and `stderr` (opened through `_jobRunnerDeps.openLog`, closed through `_jobRunnerDeps.closeLog` after the spawn); `chat-jobs/` is created first. The runner calls `unref()` on the child. Notices are built with `buildJobNotice`. In this story `attach()` resolves without reading the store, `logTail` returns `""`, polling is not armed, `dispose()` does nothing, and `cancel` / `canCancel` reject with `CHAT_JOB_NOT_RUNNING`; US-006 and US-007 fill them in. Tests go in a new `test/unit/chat/jobs/job-runner.test.ts`, with a stubbed spawn whose `exited` promise the test resolves, stubbed `kill` and `isProcessAlive`, and fake timers from `makeFakeClock()`.

`start` creates `chat-jobs/` (recursive), opens the log fd, spawns, closes the fd in the parent, registers the job in memory synchronously right after the spawn returns (so `running()` holds it before any further await), then writes the record (state `running`) and emits a change. A spawn that throws rejects with `NaxError` `CHAT_JOB_SPAWN_FAILED`, message `could not start nax <kind>: <message>`, and writes no record. When the owned child exits, the runner resolves the outcome with the observed exit code, writes the record with `endedAt`, queues a notice and emits a change. The owner always writes: it holds the real exit code.

Out of process. Each launch spawns the nax CLI as a child with `Bun.spawn`, never in process: `nax run` has no in-process API, a run takes process locks and installs signal handlers, and a crashing run must not take the chat down. Detached and headless. The child is spawned with `detached: true` and `stdin: "ignore"`. Its stdout and stderr share ONE file descriptor: the runner creates `chat-jobs/` (recursive), opens the job's log once with `node:fs` `openSync(path, "a")`, passes that same numeric fd as both `stdout` and `stderr`, and closes it in the parent after the spawn returns. Two `Bun.file()` targets would open the file twice with separate offsets and overwrite each other (verified on Bun 1.4.4). `openSync` / `closeSync` are not on the forbidden list (only `readFileSync` / `writeFileSync` are). The runner calls `unref()` on the child, so a kept job outlives the chat. Only `nax run` takes `--headless`. `nax plan` and `nax bot` do not accept the flag and are already headless without a TTY.

Every error the module throws is a `NaxError` with `stage: "chat"` and a code (`CHAT_JOB_SPAWN_FAILED`, `CHAT_JOB_NOT_RUNNING`, `CHAT_JOB_UNVERIFIED`); `check:nax-error` has a zero baseline. Notices are FIFO. A seam AC drives the stubbed owned child's exit over a real matching status.json and checks persisted terminal state; notices use the real builder prefix.

**Scope** — In: the complete runner contract and owned lifecycle, snapshot queries, subscriptions and notices. Out: implement attach/poll/logTail/dispose in US-006 and cancellation in US-007; host composition in US-013. The interface comments describe the final contract; use the explicit temporary behavior above for this story's unfinished methods. Adding job cost to the chat session's `costUsd`; the two ledgers stay separate.

**Interface** —
```ts
export const JOB_POLL_MS = 2_000;
export const CANCEL_GRACE_MS = 10_000;
export const JOB_LOG_TAIL_LINES = 40;

export interface JobRunnerOptions {
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly outputDir: string;
  readonly now: () => Date;
}
export interface StartJobRequest {
  readonly kind: ChatJobKind;
  readonly target: string;
  readonly argv: readonly string[];
  readonly sessionId: string;
  readonly approvedAt: string;
}
export interface ChatJobs {
  start(request: StartJobRequest): Promise<ChatJob>;
  /** Every job of this repo the runner knows, newest first. */
  list(): readonly ChatJob[];
  get(id: string): ChatJob | undefined;
  /** Jobs in state `running`, newest first. */
  running(): readonly ChatJob[];
  /** Running jobs this runner spawned (not re-attached ones). */
  owned(): readonly ChatJob[];
  /**
   * Persists `cancelRequested: true` to the record, sends SIGINT, then SIGTERM after
   * CANCEL_GRACE_MS if the pid is still alive. No ask. Rejects `CHAT_JOB_UNVERIFIED` for a
   * re-attached job it cannot tie to its pid (see below).
   */
  cancel(id: string): Promise<ChatJob>;
  /** The check `cancel` runs, without signalling: running, and owned or a verified re-attached run. */
  canCancel(id: string): Promise<{ readonly ok: true } | { readonly ok: false; readonly message: string }>;
  /** Loads this repo's records once; idempotent. */
  attach(): Promise<void>;
  logTail(id: string, lines?: number): Promise<string>;
  /** The oldest queued notice, left in the queue. */
  peekNotice(): string | undefined;
  /** Returns and removes the oldest queued notice. */
  takeNotice(): string | undefined;
  onChange(listener: () => void): () => void;
  /** Stops polling and timers; never kills a child. */
  dispose(): void;
}
export function createJobRunner(options: JobRunnerOptions): ChatJobs;
export const _jobRunnerDeps: { spawn; kill; isProcessAlive; setTimeout; clearTimeout; openLog; closeLog };
```

**Goal** — Add identity-checked cancellation with persisted intent and a cancellable signal escalation timer.

**Motivation** — That stitching is the pain the roadmap exists to remove.

**Approach** — `createJobRunner` gains `canCancel(id)` and `cancel(id)`, as described under Design § Data shapes: `cancel` runs the same check as `canCancel`, persists `cancelRequested: true` to the record, sends SIGINT, and arms the `CANCEL_GRACE_MS` timer that sends SIGTERM when the pid is still alive; a re-attached job passes only as a `run` whose `status.json` `run.pid` matches. `dispose()` also clears the grace timers. Tests go in a new `test/unit/chat/jobs/job-runner-cancel.test.ts`, with the same stubs and fake clock.

Re-attached identity: `isProcessAlive` cannot tell a recycled pid from the job. A re-attached job is cancellable only when it is a `run` job whose `status.json` `run.pid` equals the job's pid. Any other re-attached job rejects `cancel` with `CHAT_JOB_UNVERIFIED`, message `job <id> was started by another chat and its pid cannot be verified; stop it from that chat or by hand`.

A non-running job makes `canCancel` return `{ ok: false, message: "job <id> is not running (state <state>)" }`; `cancel` rejects `CHAT_JOB_NOT_RUNNING`. Both use `NaxError` with `stage: "chat"` for thrown errors. `jobs.cancel` on a pid that is already dead: SIGINT failure is ignored; the next poll or exit finalizes the job. No ask occurs inside the runner: provider/host callers own approval. `CANCEL_GRACE_MS = 10_000`. The cancelled outcome takes precedence over command records, preserving matching run cost/progress through the existing resolver.

**Scope** — In: replace the cancel/canCancel placeholders, verify re-attached run identity, persist intent before signalling, timer escalation and cleanup. Out: approval cards for cancel (US-011), close prompt (US-013/US-015). Sending SIGTERM after the cancel grace period when the chat has already exited; the SIGINT is sent, and the grace timer dies with the chat. Detecting a recycled pid for re-attached liveness remains excluded.

**Interface** —
```ts
/**
 * Persists `cancelRequested: true` to the record, sends SIGINT, then SIGTERM after
 * CANCEL_GRACE_MS if the pid is still alive. No ask. Rejects `CHAT_JOB_UNVERIFIED` for a
 * re-attached job it cannot tie to its pid (see below).
 */
cancel(id: string): Promise<ChatJob>;
/** The check `cancel` runs, without signalling: running, and owned or a verified re-attached run. */
canCancel(id: string): Promise<{ readonly ok: true } | { readonly ok: false; readonly message: string }>;
/** Stops polling and timers; never kills a child. */
dispose(): void;
```

**Goal** — Add host-owned approval promises that always ask, even in full mode, and fail closed on timeout, abort or reset.

**Motivation** — The standing rule is that a billed launch needs the user's explicit yes at the launch moment (user).

**Approach** — `src/chat/ask-controller.ts` gains `HostApprovalCard`, `DEFAULT_HOST_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_MS`, the `approvalTimeoutMs` and `now` options, and `requestHostApproval`. Host requests use request ids starting `host-`. They join `pending()` as `approval_requested` events with `expiresAt = now + approvalTimeoutMs`. `handle()` never answers them, in either mode. `canRemember()` is false for them. `resolve()` routes them to the waiting promise and never to `options.answer`: `allow_once` and `allow_session` give `"allow"`, `deny` gives `"deny"`, and none adds to approval memory. A timer (`_askControllerDeps.setTimeout` / `clearTimeout`) resolves `"deny"` at expiry. An abort of `card.signal` resolves `"deny"`. `reset()` resolves every open host request `"deny"`. Each resolution removes the event from `pending()` and notifies listeners. Tests go in the existing `test/unit/chat/ask-controller.test.ts`, plus a fallback test in the existing `test/unit/chat/ask-fallback.test.ts`.

Launch approval. A `static` provider tool gets no framework ask, and `ChatAskController.handle()` auto-allows every session approval in full mode (`src/chat/ask-controller.ts:53`). So the controller gains a separate host entry point, `requestHostApproval(card)`. It adds a synthesized `approval_requested` event to `pending()`, so the existing card (`src/tui/chat/ask-card.ts`) and the Telegram fallback (`ask-fallback.ts`) show it unchanged. It is never auto-allowed, never remembered, and resolves `deny` on timeout, on the tool call's abort signal, or on `reset()`.

Options gain `approvalTimeoutMs?: number` (default `DEFAULT_HOST_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_MS = 600_000`) and `now?: () => Date`. New `_askControllerDeps = { setTimeout, clearTimeout }` holds the expiry timer (the documented `setTimeout` exception: the handle is cancelled when the ask resolves). Preserve ordinary session approval behavior and positive approval memory.

Required fixture adaptation: add an inert `requestHostApproval: async () => "deny"` member to the literal `asks` in `test/ui/chat/chat-app.test.tsx` and `test/ui/chat/slash-commands.test.tsx`, and the literal `ChatAskController` built by `fakeController` in `test/ui/chat/ask-card.test.tsx`. The new method is required; no existing assertions change.

**Scope** — In: host-card controller lifecycle, timers, fallback compatibility and these literal fixture adaptations. Out: job launch/cancel cards (US-009/US-011), passing resolved config timeout (US-013), UI close gating (US-015). Full-run (chain) approval and the agent starting a second billed step without a new card; this is feature `chat-workflow-chain`. Cross-session memory of rulings or preferences; this is its own slice, not yet designed (user ruling 2026-10-27).

**Interface** —
```ts
export interface HostApprovalCard {
  readonly tool: string;
  readonly summary: string;
  readonly command?: string;
  readonly reason: string;
  readonly signal?: AbortSignal;
}
export const DEFAULT_HOST_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_MS = 600_000;
```
`ChatAskController` adds `requestHostApproval(card: HostApprovalCard): Promise<"allow" | "deny">`; `ChatAskControllerOptions` retains `{ mode, answer }` and gains `approvalTimeoutMs?: number` and `now?: () => Date`.

**Goal** — Add the nax-jobs provider's four billed launch tools with target validation, launch cards and job-start results.

**Motivation** — The standing rule is that a billed launch needs the user's explicit yes at the launch moment (user).

**Approach** — New `src/chat/jobs/jobs-launch.ts` (input parse, target checks, card, start; `_jobsLaunchDeps`) and new `src/chat/jobs/jobs-provider.ts` with `JobsProviderOptions` and `createJobsProvider`, exposing the `plan`, `run`, `bot_review` and `bot_fix` tools as in Design § The `nax-jobs` provider, launch steps 1, 2, 4, 5 and the `jobs.start` call of step 6. A `loadPRD` rejection is left to propagate in this story; US-010 maps it to the unreadable-`prd.json` error. The busy checks (step 3 and the re-check in step 6) and the `start`-rejection mapping are US-010. In this story the provider has exactly these four tools and no `instructions`; US-011 adds the rest. Tests go in a new `test/unit/chat/jobs/jobs-launch.test.ts`, with a temp-dir fixture repo, a stub `approve`, and a stub `ChatJobs` whose `start` records its request.

The launch logic lives in `src/chat/jobs/jobs-launch.ts`, with `_jobsLaunchDeps = { git, lockHolder, exists, loadPRD }`. `lockHolder(repoRoot)` reads `<repoRoot>/nax.lock` and returns its `pid` when that pid is alive, else `null` (a missing or unparseable file is `null`); its real implementation and use are US-010. Use gitWithTimeout for the git seam. Tool input schemas (`additionalProperties: false` on each): plan properties `feature: string`, `spec: string`, `profiles: string[]`, required `feature`; run properties `feature: string`, `profiles: string[]`, `maxIterations: integer`, `maxCost: number`, required `feature`; bot_review properties `pr: integer`, `profiles: string[]`, `model: string`, `decisions: string`, required `pr`; bot_fix properties `pr: integer`, `profiles: string[]`, `model: string`, `trust: boolean`, `noRereview: boolean`, required `pr`. The four launch tools' descriptions include `Billed` and `asks the user`.

A launch, in this order (busy steps are added by US-010):
1. `parseJobInput` (validation table in Failure Handling). For plan and run, `featureDir(repoRoot, f)` is then called; when it throws, its message is the error result (US-010 adds that mapping).
2. Target checks. plan: when `spec` is absent, it resolves to `.nax/features/<f>/spec.md` if that file exists, else `.nax/specs/<f>.md` if that exists; the resolved file must exist. run: `<featureDir>/prd.json` must exist and load. bot_review: `decisions`, when given, must exist.
4. The card: `approve({ tool: "nax-jobs__<kind>", summary, command, reason, signal: ctx.signal })`.
   - `summary`: `nax plan <f>`, `nax run <f>`, `nax bot review #<pr>`, `nax bot fix #<pr>`.
   - `command`: the argv joined with single spaces.
   - `reason`: lines joined with `\n`, in this order:
     - `workdir <repoRoot>`, plus ` on branch <b>` when `git symbolic-ref --short HEAD` exits 0;
     - `nax <naxEntry joined with spaces>`;
     - `profiles <p1, p2>`, or `profiles (none)`;
     - run only: `stories <pending> pending of <total>; max iterations <m>`, where `m` is `maxIterations` from the input or else the provider's resolved value;
     - run only, when `pending > m`: `warning: <pending> stories pending but max iterations is <m>; pass maxIterations (nax#2706)`;
     - run only, when `interactionPlugin` is `"cli"`: `warning: this run has no channel for its own asks; they will time out`;
     - plan only, when `<featureDir>/prd.json` exists: `warning: prd.json exists and will be replaced`.
5. `deny` → an error result: `[chat] the user declined <summary>; do not retry with different arguments unless the user asks`.
6. `allow` → `jobs.start({ kind, target, argv, sessionId: sessionId(), approvedAt: now().toISOString() })`. The result content is one line of JSON `{ "job": <id>, "state": "running", "log": <logPath> }`, followed by a newline and `A [nax-jobs] notice arrives when it ends.`

Target failures: plan spec not found → `[chat] no spec for <f>; looked for <path(s)>`; run with no prd.json → `[chat] no prd.json for <f>; run nax plan first`; bot_review decisions missing → `[chat] decisions file <path> not found`. Parse failures return `{ isError: true, content: <parse error> }`. Resolve a spec to a repo-relative path before building argv. Reuse loadPRD/countStories from @/prd. The provider id is `nax-jobs`, kind `static`, stages `["*"]`; approval is explicitly host-owned, never inferred from static-provider framework behavior.

**Scope** — In: exactly four launch tools, schemas, parse/target/card/start flow, summaries/reasons/results and abort-signal propagation. Out: busy checks, real lockHolder, featureDir/loadPRD/start rejection mapping (US-010), list/cancel/instructions (US-011), host registration (US-013). Routing the run's own asks into the chat card, fixing nax#2706, changing the child commands or allowing subagents to launch billed work are excluded.

**Interface** —
```ts
